 its later study by Gopala, Hemachandra, and Fibonacci, and closes with Exercise Set 8.1 (six questions on explicit-rule term-finding, term verification, and a three-term recursive rule). Section 8.4 defines and explains the arithmetic progression (AP), introduced through a growing pattern of stages where four squares are added at each stage, producing the sequence 1, 5, 9, 13, ... ; the section derives the explicit rule tn = 1 + (n-1) x 4 = 4n - 3 for this pattern, identifies the constant difference between consecutive terms as the defining property of an AP, generalises to the standard form a, a+d, a+2d, ... with first term a and common difference d (positive or negative, illustrated with the decreasing AP 11, 7, 3, -1, -5, ...), and gives the general explicit rule tn = a + (n-1)d. Section 8.4.1 visualises an AP by tabulating stage number against the number of squares, plotting the resulting ordered pairs, and observing that they lie on a straight line, then states the recursive rule for an AP (t1 = a, tn = tn-1 + d for n >= 2) and closes with Example 5, which models a taxi fare with a Rs.200 booking fee plus Rs.40 per kilometre as the AP 240, 280, 320, ... and derives its nth term 200 + 40n. Section 8.5 derives the formula for the sum of the first n natural numbers: it first computes the sum of the first ten natural numbers by writing the sum forwards and backwards and pairing terms that each total 11, generalises this reverse-and-add argument algebraically to obtain Sn = n(n+1)/2, restates the same result pictorially via a dot diagram showing a rectangular array split by a zigzag partition, applies the formula to compute a sum of consecutive integers (25 to 58) by differencing two partial sums, attributes the earliest known statement of the result to Aryabhata's Aryabhatiya, and connects the formula back to the triangular-number sequence by showing that its nth term equals n(n+1)/2; the section closes with Exercise Set 8.2 (seven questions on AP terms, term position, recursive rules, a system of two linear equations for a and d, counting and summing multiples, and a salary-increment and marble-arrangement word problem). Section 8.6 defines and explains the geometric progression (GP), introduced through a growing pattern of stages where the number of green squares doubles at each stage, producing the sequence 3, 6, 12, 24, ... ; the section derives the explicit rule tn = 3 x 2^(n-1), identifies the constant ratio between consecutive terms (the common ratio) as the defining property of a GP, generalises to the standard form a, ar, ar^2, ... with first term a and common ratio r, gives the general explicit rule tn = ar^(n-1), and works through Example 6, Example 7, Example 8 (testing whether given sequences are GPs and finding their common ratios) and Example 9 (verifying that 5, 15/4, 45/16, 135/64, ... is a GP with r = 3/4 by computing three consecutive term ratios, then writing its nth term), closing with two inline exercises testing further sequences for the GP property and finding a recursive rule for tn = 3 x 10^(n-1). Section 8.6.1 explores the Sierpinski triangle fractal, constructed by repeatedly removing the central triangle from each black triangle to produce Stages 0, 1, 2, 3, ..., establishes that the number of black triangles (1, 3, 9, 27, ...) forms a GP with explicit rule 3^n and recursive rule t1 = 1, tn = 3 x tn-1, separately establishes that the shaded area of the black region forms a different GP with explicit rule (3/4)^n and recursive rule s1 = 1, sn = (3/4) x sn-1 (since each stage retains 3/4 of the previous stage's area), consolidates both rules in a summary table (Table 1), explains the general concept of a fractal as a self-similar pattern repeating at every scale and gives natural examples (tree branching, cauliflower/broccoli, snowflakes, coastlines), and introduces Wacław Sierpiński as the mathematician for whom the fractal is named. Section 8.6.2 visualises a GP by tabulating stage number against